EXHIBIT B
FULL TEXT BALLOT PROPOSITION
OF THE SAN BERNARDINO MOUNTAINS COMMUNITY HOSPITAL DISTRICT
BOND MEASURE ELECTION NOVEMBER 3, 2026
The following is the full proposition presented to the voters by the San Bernardino Mountains
Community Hospital District.
“To repair and upgrade 75-year-old Mountains Community Hospital including: fixing deteriorating
roofs, plumbing, electrical, and safety systems; upgrading/expanding emergency department, medical
technology, and treatments; and maintaining local access to life-saving emergency medical care for
accidents, heart attacks, strokes, and other emergencies, shall San Bernardino Mountains Community
Hospital District’s measure be adopted authorizing $61,000,000 in bonds at legal rates, levying $25
per $100,000 assessed value ($3,500,000 annually) while bonds are outstanding, with independent
oversight and all money locally-controlled?”
PROJECT LIST
The Board of Directors of the San Bernardino Mountains Community Hospital District has
evaluated the District’s urgent and critical facility needs in developing the scope of projects to be
funded, including evaluating alternative funding sources. The proposed Project consists of updates,
improvements and expansions to the District’s facility located at 29101 Hospital Road, Lake
Arrowhead, in an unincorporated area of San Bernardino County (the “Hospital”), to meet SB 1953
requirements by January 1, 2030. In 2025, the District issued Bond Anticipation Notes as construction
financing, which mature in 2028, to finance a portion of the Project. A portion of the proceeds of the
proposed Bonds will be used by the District to refinance the Bond Anticipation Notes at or before their
maturity.
The Project is being constructed in phases and will ultimately include:
• Construction of a new replacement inpatient acute care wing and emergency
department to the Hospital building,
• A new entry, drop-off and pick-up area, to improve patient access to the Hospital,
• Upgrades and renovation of the existing inpatient acute care unit for use as long term
care (skilled nursing) beds,
• Infrastructure, utility, and system upgrades,
• Grading and other construction and improvements to the existing receiving and parking
areas located adjacent to the Hospital’s main entrance and medical office building, to
improve patient access to the Hospital, and
• Seismic upgrades to keep patients, skilled nursing care residents, doctors, nurses, and
other employees and visitors safe at the Hospital and keep the hospital fully functional
with temporary power, water and wastewater systems for up to 72 hours if cut off from
external utilities during emergency situations.
